WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:03.139
The M1 Abrams entered service in 1980.

00:00:03.139 --> 00:00:09.160
A fast, heavily armored tank with the very
latest technologies to give it every advantage

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:10.840
on the battlefield.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.590
First seeing action in Operation Desert Storm.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:19.490
The plain arid camouflaged tank gained a reputation
quickly in the barren deserts of Saudi Arabia

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:21.010
and Kuwait.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:26.080
Powered by a high speed turbine engine, the
M1 raced across this difficult terrain to

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:29.230
liberate Kuwait from Iraqi occupation.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:35.649
The german designed 120 mm smoothbore cannon
had longer range, more accurate fire and more

00:00:35.649 --> 00:00:37.420
advanced ammunition.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:42.539
It easily dispatched the lower tech soviet
supplied tanks of the Iraqi Army

00:00:42.539 --> 00:00:48.559
Sometime this year it’s expected that the
US will deliver a battalion of 31 M1 Abram

00:00:48.559 --> 00:00:55.040
tanks to Ukraine to once again face off against
the Soviet tanks it was designed to battle.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:00.170
The Ukrainian Army will need training to maintain
and operate these new tanks, because it’s

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:02.059
unlike any other tank.

00:01:02.059 --> 00:01:07.270
And although this tank are over 40 years old
it's still more than capable of holding its

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:13.470
own on the battlefield, thanks to continual
modernisation and forward thinking design.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:24.270
This is the insane engineering of the M1 Abrams.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:29.630
The M1 Abrams, upon engine start up, sounds
more like a military aircraft getting ready

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:31.310
to take off.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:41.600
*minor pause 1-3 seconds to allow sound to
be heard*

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.820
A terrifying increasing pitch as the engine
revs up to speed.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:51.610
The whirring sound emanating from the rotating
turbine blades hidden inside.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:57.000
The turbine engine, typically used for jet
aircraft, is an engine designed for high speed

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:02.950
operation with minimal weight, a counter intuitive
choice for a heavy battle tank.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:05.150
So why was it chosen?

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:09.230
The turbine engine would give the M1 two huge
advantages.

00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:15.400
They are much lighter than an equivalent diesel
engine, weighing just 1.1 tonnes, developing

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.629
1500 brake horsepower.

00:02:17.629 --> 00:02:24.150
The V12 diesel engine of the Challenger 2
produces just 1200 brake horsepower with 2

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:25.530
tonnes of metal.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:30.640
This compactness and extreme high power to
weight ratio allows the M1 to stack on layers

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:35.190
of armor without sacrificing acceleration
or top speed.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:42.420
We can plot torque vs shaft speed of the HoneyWell
AGT1500 turbine engine of the M1 against the

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:50.360
V12 MTU 883 of the Challenger 2E, and we can
see the turbine has a massive torque advantage

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:52.540
at lower shaft speeds.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:56.700
In its lightest configuration M1 weighs 61.8
tonnes.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:00.099
A typical toyota corolla weighs 1.1 tonne.

00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:06.300
That’s equivalent to 56 Toyota Corollas,
yet this thing can still accelerate from 0-32

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:13.110
kilometers per hour in just 7 seconds with
a top speed of 72 kilometers per hour.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:18.650
The M1 Abrams primarily uses diesel fuel,
as diesel can act as an additional layer of

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:20.049
armor.

00:03:20.049 --> 00:03:25.069
Liquids are extremely good at absorbing energy
from explosions and kinetic energy weapons,

00:03:25.069 --> 00:03:30.689
they are most effective against shaped charges,
and tanks carry a lot of liquid in the form

00:03:30.689 --> 00:03:31.750
of fuel.

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:36.640
It may seem counterintuitive to use a fuel
as protection from an explosion, but diesel

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.770
fuel is not very flammable.

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:44.230
In fact if you throw a lit match into a puddle
of diesel it will put out the match.

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:49.050
Diesel engines require immense pressure and
a sustained flame to ignite.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:53.689
Because of this diesel can actually be used
with relative safety as armor.

00:03:53.689 --> 00:03:58.129
However the turbine engine can also operate
on most fuel types.

00:03:58.129 --> 00:04:01.810
This is a huge advantage in military logistics.

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:05.470
Making joint operations with NATO vastly easier.

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:07.590
In the years following World War 2.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:11.430
The Soviets favored diesel compression ignition
engines.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:17.000
The western Allies used a mixture of both
gasoline spark ignition and diesel compression

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:18.170
ignition.

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:22.580
Transporting fuel is one of the largest logistic
challenges in war.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:26.300
Needing several types of fuel makes it all
the more difficult.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:31.669
The M1 can run on marine diesel, gasoline
or even kerosene if needed.

00:04:31.669 --> 00:04:36.199
Which not only makes it easier to procure
fuel in a warzone, but comes with the added

00:04:36.199 --> 00:04:40.740
bonus of helping the M1 Abram operate in hot
or cold weather.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:45.190
Dealing with the wild swings in temperature
that Ukraine can expect throughout the year

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:46.389
with ease.

00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:51.650
Where diesel fuels can crystallize at low
temperatures, kerosene can be used instead.

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:57.220
The turbine engine of the M1 works similarly
to aircraft jet engines, with some important

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:58.410
differences.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:03.440
Air enters the engine here, where it is compressed
by the low pressure compressor, and then the

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:08.600
high pressure compressor, each individually
driven by separate turbine stages.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:13.310
These turbine stages are driven by the combustor,
which works a little differently to typical

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:14.990
aircraft combustors.

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:19.650
It is mounted perpendicularly to the engine,
and protrudes out of the engine.

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:24.759
This makes maintenance access to the combustor
easier, with only a simple bolted cover needing

00:05:24.759 --> 00:05:25.759
to be removed.

00:05:25.759 --> 00:05:28.569
There are two drive shafts in this engine.

00:05:28.569 --> 00:05:33.180
A secondary drive shaft, driven by the high
pressure turbine, which runs forward to an

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:38.190
accessory gearbox that runs things like compressors,
electronics and hydraulics.

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:43.060
The main drive shaft, driven by a dedicated
power turbine, which is not connected to the

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:46.790
compressors, runs rearward to the tanks drive
sprockets.

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:50.830
This primary driveshaft and its reduction
gearbox is surrounded by something called

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:52.420
a recuperator.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:57.479
This is the biggest difference between this
turbine engine and a typical aircraft jet

00:05:57.479 --> 00:05:58.479
engine.

00:05:58.479 --> 00:06:03.890
We don’t want the hot exhaust of the engine
spewing out like a jet engine, the heat signature

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:09.000
this would create would be a giant beacon
for heat guided missiles and give the tanks

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:12.770
location to any enemy using thermal vision.

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:15.770
We also don’t want to waste all that heat
energy.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:19.280
The recuperator is essentially a giant heat
exchanger.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:23.990
Air coming from the compressor stage is passed
through it, where it is heated by the exhaust

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:26.630
before entering the combustion chamber.

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:31.729
This lowers the heat signature of the exhaust
and increases fuel efficiency, transferring

00:06:31.729 --> 00:06:37.040
more heat energy back into the engine instead
of losing it to the atmosphere.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:41.950
But the M1 Abrams is still a thirsty machine,
even by tank standards.

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:46.819
The turbine engine uses twice the fuel as
a comparable diesel engine per kilometer.

00:06:46.819 --> 00:06:55.630
Where the M1A1 consumes around while cruising
at 40 kilometers per hour, the Leopard 2 consumes

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:57.180
2.2 liters per kilometer.

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:00.370
[REF] [1]
But the US military deemed this issue a cost

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:04.300
worth paying for the capabilities the turbine
engine provided.

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:10.110
Allowing the M1 to carry an obscene amount
of armor, while keeping its acceleration high.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:16.190
The armor installed on the M1 Abrams has evolved
and changed over the last 4 decades.

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:22.430
The exact details of its thickness, location,
materials and layering is classified for obvious

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:27.060
reasons, but there is a great deal known about
the science and nature of its armor.

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:32.669
It is well publicized that early M1 variants
used a type of composite ceramic armor called

00:07:32.669 --> 00:07:33.669
Chobham.

00:07:33.669 --> 00:07:40.680
Which derives much of its ballistic resistance
from an extremely hard and light ceramic layer.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:45.289
Hardness in material science is a measure
of a material's ability to resist localized

00:07:45.289 --> 00:07:47.770
deformation, like a scratch.

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:52.580
Diamonds are extremely hard, and because of
this industrial diamonds are coated onto cutting

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:56.889
tools to help them cut through materials,
without being eroded themselves.

00:07:56.889 --> 00:08:01.560
A hard material can scratch and erode a softer
material.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:06.310
Hardness can be measured with a vickers hardness
test, which pushes a pyramid shaped diamond

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:07.660
into the material.

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:12.490
The hardness is then calculated by dividing
the force applied by the resulting surface

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:14.970
area of the indentation.

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:21.370
The rolled homogeneous armor steel of world
war 2, has a Vickers Hardness of 380, a high

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:27.930
carbon hard steel is about 550, while a ceramic
like silicone carbide offers a hardness 5

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:33.300
times greater, up to 2500 [2] While being
much lighter than steel.

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:35.620
Making it an excellent candidate for armor.

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:40.750
But, anyone that has dropped a dinner plate
knows that ceramics are extremely brittle.

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:44.130
They shatter into a million pieces with little
force,

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:48.760
but this can be used to the tanks advantage
when combined with a tougher metal backing

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:49.760
plating.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:53.910
In this configuration the ceramic is placed
on the outside of the metal plating, acting

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:56.450
like an extremely hard outer shell.

00:08:56.450 --> 00:09:00.930
When a round strikes the armor the compressive
strength and hardness of the ceramic coating

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:05.940
causes the round to fracture and break apart,
at the same time the ceramic coating begins

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:11.410
to fracture and fragment, spreading the energy
of impact across a larger area which is then

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:15.410
absorbed by the tougher metal plate backing
the ceramic.

00:09:15.410 --> 00:09:20.630
Tough in material science meaning it can absorb
a lot of energy without fracturing, the opposite

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:22.010
of brittle.

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:26.700
Further research and experimentation found
that ceramic armor performed even better when

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:28.510
placed under compression.

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:33.490
This can be achieved by simply adding a face
plate and bolting the two pieces together.

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:39.180
This changes the dynamics of an impact significantly,
and helps immensely with resisting attack

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:41.890
from long-rod kinetic energy projectiles.

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:42.890
[REF][3]

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:45.240
These are rounds specifically designed as
anti-armor weapons.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:50.910
They are thin, long dart-like projectiles
that require a sabot to launch out of tank

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:51.910
barrels.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:55.470
They are typically manufactured from high
density materials like Tungsten.

00:09:55.470 --> 00:10:00.750
With a thin aerodynamic shape and high density,
these weapons have an extremely high ballistic

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:06.060
coefficient, allowing them to ram into targets
at a distance at high velocities.

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:09.800
They can obliterate rows of concrete walls
with ease.

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:14.590
Embedded compressed ceramic armor can defeat
these kinetic energy weapons.

00:10:14.590 --> 00:10:15.590
[REF][4]

00:10:15.590 --> 00:10:19.590
When the long rod projectile strikes the face
plate it sends a pressure wave through the

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:23.440
ceramic that pulverizes it and increases its
volume.

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:28.490
This creates what is essentially an abrasive
maze of extremely hard and sharp particles

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:32.490
the penetrator has to push through, gradually
grinding it away.

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:33.490
[REF][5]

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:37.829
Computer simulations of this effect show that
the harder the material the better.

00:10:37.829 --> 00:10:40.209
This is the magic of ceramic composite armor.

00:10:40.209 --> 00:10:41.209
[REF] [6]

00:10:41.209 --> 00:10:45.769
On top of these metal and ceramic layers there
is typically a very dense inner liner called

00:10:45.769 --> 00:10:47.709
a spall lining.

00:10:47.709 --> 00:10:52.060
Projectiles don’t necessarily need to penetrate
every layer of armor to be deadly.

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:56.639
If they hit with enough force the kinetic
energy can simply transfer through the material

00:10:56.639 --> 00:11:01.950
as a wave and cause material on the inside
of the tank to splitter and turn into deadly

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:05.019
shrapnel inside the crew compartment.

00:11:05.019 --> 00:11:06.680
This is called spall.

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:09.899
Some munitions are specifically designed to
cause this.

00:11:09.899 --> 00:11:14.910
High explosive squash heads are made from
soft plastic explosives that spread out over

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:16.520
the armor's surface.

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:21.050
With the increased surface area and direct
contact with the armor, the explosion transfers

00:11:21.050 --> 00:11:26.120
a great deal of energy through the material
and blows out the armor's backing.

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:30.180
This works by sending a compressive shock
wave through the material and reflects and

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:35.810
rebounds inside the material, creating regions
of intense stress that fractures the armor.

00:11:35.810 --> 00:11:40.410
The spall liner is a ductile and dense material
that limits spalling.

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:47.530
For early M1s this layer was typically composed
of lead, but beginning in 1988 certain M1A1s,

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:52.990
began to be upgraded with depleted uranium
spall liners, which are even denser than lead.

00:11:52.990 --> 00:11:57.880
And all new M1A2s were assembled with depleted
uranium liners.

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:02.590
However, modern composite armor makes it difficult
for this shock wave to transmit through the

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:09.060
material and a spaced layer with an air gap
can defeat this squash head munition completely.

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:13.630
Additional reactive armor tiles can be added
to the outside of the M1 too.

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:17.750
Reactive armor is particularly effective at
dealing with shaped charges.

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:22.740
Shaped charges consist of a charge shaped
with a hollow indentation, lined with a ductile

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:24.130
metal liner.

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:29.050
When the charge is detonated a pressure wave
forms behind this metal liner, deforming it

00:12:29.050 --> 00:12:32.940
and accelerating the metal into a lance stream
of particles.

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:37.690
The shaped charge effectively creates a hypersonic
projectile at point black range.

00:12:37.690 --> 00:12:41.320
[REF][7] It’s highly effective at cutting
through armor.

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:46.050
Reactive armor works by placing an explosive
charge between two metal plates.

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:51.250
When a jet from a shaped charge strikes the
upper plate it detonates the inner explosive.

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:55.910
You may think this could damage the tank,
but tank’s lower armor is more than capable

00:12:55.910 --> 00:13:01.010
of dealing with the relatively blunt pressure
formed by the reactive armor detonation.

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:06.199
The outer plate then flies outwards to disrupt
the incoming jet while the shockwave formed

00:13:06.199 --> 00:13:10.820
by the detonation also breaks up the stream
of metal approaching the tank.

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:16.279
Ofcourse, the best defense is to not be hit
at all, and the M1 can create a smoke screen

00:13:16.279 --> 00:13:18.190
for itself when needed.

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:23.190
The M1 has two systems for generating smoke
to conceal itself in an engagement.

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:28.889
The first involves simply spraying fuel into
the engine exhaust, which vaporises the fuel

00:13:28.889 --> 00:13:32.570
and creates a large opaque cloud behind the
tank.

00:13:32.570 --> 00:13:37.090
However it’s extremely important that the
driver remembers which fuel the tank is running

00:13:37.090 --> 00:13:38.090
on.

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:43.589
This works for diesel fuel, but if gasoline
or kerosene is used it won’t conceal the

00:13:43.589 --> 00:13:46.170
M1, it will set it on fire.

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:50.740
The second system uses these grenade launchers
mounted on the outside of the turret.

00:13:50.740 --> 00:13:53.180
There are two versions that the M1 uses.

00:13:53.180 --> 00:14:00.220
The 8 canister M257 typically used with US
Marine Corps M1s, and the more widely used

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:01.899
6 can M250.

00:14:01.899 --> 00:14:05.329
These launchers are controlled from the tank
commander's seat here.

00:14:05.329 --> 00:14:10.090
Pressing 1 button launches 6 grenades, 3 from
the left and right side.

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:13.339
Pressing both buttons launches all 12 of the
grenades.

00:14:13.339 --> 00:14:17.690
Launching them about 30 meters from the tank
and providing a shrouding curtain of smoke

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:19.350
to hide it’s movements.

00:14:19.350 --> 00:14:24.399
The engineers of the M1 Abrams did everything
in their power to make the M1 as survivable

00:14:24.399 --> 00:14:26.710
as possible, protecting the crew inside.

00:14:26.710 --> 00:14:31.190
And the highly trained crew are the most important
part of this machine.

00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:35.550
The M1 Abrams does not have an automatic loader,
like many modern tanks.

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:41.079
It has a dedicated crew member, the loader,
to load rounds into the breach.

00:14:41.079 --> 00:14:45.630
Autoloaders are a feature of modern Russian
tanks, however the USA has shunned them in

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:52.110
their tanks, seeing an autoloader as an unnecessarily
complex mechanism that would impact the M1s

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:53.110
reliability.

00:14:53.110 --> 00:14:57.370
Seeing a forth crew member, capable of keeping
watch, maintaining the vehicle and taking

00:14:57.370 --> 00:15:02.670
over responsibilities in an emergency as an
advantage, not a disadvantage.

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:07.259
The loader enters the tank through the turret
hatch, sitting to the left of the main gun

00:15:07.259 --> 00:15:12.070
with access to the ammunition box behind them
. The gunner to their right is in charge of

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:17.649
aiming at targets using these day and thermal
night vision sights, along with a laser range

00:15:17.649 --> 00:15:22.130
finder to input target distances into the
ballistics computer.

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:26.340
When instructed by the tank commander, sitting
behind the gunner, the loader will press a

00:15:26.340 --> 00:15:31.209
switch with their knee to open a hydraulically
actuated armored door behind them.

00:15:31.209 --> 00:15:35.390
The tank commander will specify the round
needed and the loader will take it out and

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:40.410
load it into the breach, close the breach
and move the safe handle into the armed position.

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:45.360
This process will be repeated until a ceasefire
is called by the tank commander.

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:50.300
The armored ammunition access door is only
open when loading, and this is crucial for

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:52.810
the survivability of the M1 Abrams.

00:15:52.810 --> 00:15:58.240
If a round penetrates the ammunition box it
can result in a lethal detonation of the ammunition

00:15:58.240 --> 00:15:59.620
stored inside.

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:04.399
This armored door is capable of withstanding
this blast, and panels on top of the tank,

00:16:04.399 --> 00:16:08.889
called blow out panels, are designed to break
and allow the pressure and heat to be directed

00:16:08.889 --> 00:16:10.820
upwards and away from the crew.

00:16:10.820 --> 00:16:14.279
[REF][8]
These rounds are fired out of the M1s 120

00:16:14.279 --> 00:16:15.690
mm cannon.

00:16:15.690 --> 00:16:19.620
Early M1s were fitted with a 105 mm cannon.

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:23.110
105 referring to the bore diameter of the
gun.

00:16:23.110 --> 00:16:27.750
This decision was made primarily to allow
a sharing of ammunition and parts between

00:16:27.750 --> 00:16:32.190
the 105 mm on the previous generation M60
tank.

00:16:32.190 --> 00:16:36.430
With the development of depleted uranium rounds
it was viewed that this gun was more than

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:39.370
adequate to deal with any soviet armor.

00:16:39.370 --> 00:16:44.880
However US allies like Germany did not want
to use depleted uranium round due to ethical

00:16:44.880 --> 00:16:50.250
implications, and were moving towards 120
mm cannons with the British Chieftan tank

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:55.620
using the Royal Ordanance L11 and the German
Leopard 2 using the Rheinmetall RH-120.

00:16:55.620 --> 00:17:01.110
This posed a problem for NATO’s goals of
standardizing wherever possible to optimize

00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:02.130
logistics.

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:07.120
With ammunition factories across NATO countries
producing the same ammunition, this ensured

00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:12.870
ammunition could not only be shared, but manufactured
as close to the frontline as possible.

00:17:12.870 --> 00:17:21.220
The 120 mm guns of the M1A1 and M1A2 are in
fact the German Rheinmetall RH-120 manufactured

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:22.740
under license in the US.

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:27.510
A 5.3 metre long, 3.3 tonne smooth bore cannon.

00:17:27.510 --> 00:17:31.880
This large bulge in the middle of the cannon
is designed to help evacuate the barrel of

00:17:31.880 --> 00:17:34.650
propellant gasses after each firing.

00:17:34.650 --> 00:17:39.110
Once the round leaves the barrel atmospheric
pressure can prevent the gasses from leaving,

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:44.110
and once the breach is opened the potentially
harmful and explosive gas can enter the crew

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:45.110
compartment.

00:17:45.110 --> 00:17:50.010
The bore evacuator is a pretty simple solution,
with holes that allow gasses to enter the

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:52.790
evacuator as the round passes by.

00:17:52.790 --> 00:17:55.150
This acts like a pressure reservoir.

00:17:55.150 --> 00:17:59.370
When the round leaves the barrel with this
attachment, the pressure is released from

00:17:59.370 --> 00:18:05.340
forward facing holes at the far end of the
evacuator that pushes the remaining gas outwards.

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:10.800
The two primary rounds used by the M1 are
the M829 Depleted Uranium round, which is

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:17.720
a saboted kinetic energy round, and the M830A1
HEAT Round, which, despite its name, does

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:20.490
not use heat as part of its offensive.

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:24.090
It uses a kinetic energy shaped charge.

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:26.429
We have been piling on weight in this vehicle.

00:18:26.429 --> 00:18:31.730
The armor, cannon, engine and ammunition makes
this an extremely heavy vehicle.

00:18:31.730 --> 00:18:37.059
Weighing between 52 and 68 tonnes depending
on the generation and configuration.

00:18:37.059 --> 00:18:42.760
The M1 Abrams needed a track and suspension
system capable of bearing that weight.

00:18:42.760 --> 00:18:46.490
For a vehicle this heavy typical helical springs
have some problems.

00:18:46.490 --> 00:18:49.010
First, their maximum travel is limited.

00:18:49.010 --> 00:18:54.460
At a particular load the layers of a helical
spring will meet and no further travel is

00:18:54.460 --> 00:18:59.520
possible, bottoming out and providing an extremely
uncomfortable ride for the crew.

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:03.990
To deal with a heavier vehicle we need to
increase the spring coil diameter, taking

00:19:03.990 --> 00:19:06.669
up an increasing amount of space.

00:19:06.669 --> 00:19:12.330
This was an issue for world war 2 era tanks,
with the M4 Sherman opting to use a volute

00:19:12.330 --> 00:19:17.110
spring, which is a conical spring that is
capable of compressing to a much smaller size

00:19:17.110 --> 00:19:19.890
as the sheets of spring steel overlap.

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:24.809
However most modern tanks utilize the torsion
springs, which take up very little space inside

00:19:24.809 --> 00:19:28.330
the tank, though they do raise the tanks height
considerably.

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:33.179
The torsion springs of the M1 are located
here, and because they are inside the hull

00:19:33.179 --> 00:19:36.640
they raise the hull height by 150 millimeters.

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:41.049
The road wheel is attached to a lever that
can travel up and down, this lever arm is

00:19:41.049 --> 00:19:45.770
attached to the torsion bar so that it twists
when the lever arm moves up and down.

00:19:45.770 --> 00:19:50.240
The spring force is derived from the bars
resistance to torsion.

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:53.559
Older tanks incorporating torsion bars had
limited travel.

00:19:53.559 --> 00:19:58.630
The M1’s predecessor, the M60, had a wheel
travel of just 20 centimeters.

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:03.210
During development of the M1 the two tanks
were pitted against each other, with the M1

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:08.049
benefiting from 20 years of material science
progress, its max travel was nearly double

00:20:08.049 --> 00:20:11.539
that of the M60 at 38 centimeters.

00:20:11.539 --> 00:20:16.870
When tested on a track with a series of 30
centimeter bumps at 32 kilometers per hour.

00:20:16.870 --> 00:20:22.190
The M60 managed to break its front front wheels,
lost control, and broke the drivers arm.

00:20:22.190 --> 00:20:26.130
The torsion spring of the M1 runs along the
width of the hull.

00:20:26.130 --> 00:20:27.650
Making them quite heavy.

00:20:27.650 --> 00:20:32.470
We need the torsion spring to be as long as
possible because the travel distance is a

00:20:32.470 --> 00:20:35.299
function of the torsion bar's length.

00:20:35.299 --> 00:20:38.190
Longer torsion bars can twist more before
breaking.

00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:43.960
An interesting quirk of the torsion spring
system is that typical designs, like the M1’s,

00:20:43.960 --> 00:20:46.510
mean that the road wheels cannot be aligned.

00:20:46.510 --> 00:20:51.280
We cannot simply connect the lever arms of
opposite road wheels to the same torsion spring,

00:20:51.280 --> 00:20:55.179
as they would frequently travel together,
creating no torsion.

00:20:55.179 --> 00:20:59.980
The far end of the bar needs to be rigidly
mounted to the body of the tank in order to

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:01.730
develop a spring force.

00:21:01.730 --> 00:21:07.440
So two torsion bars need to be mounted alongside
each other, creating an offset in distance

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:09.841
between the two sides of the tracks.

00:21:09.841 --> 00:21:12.830
The two sides of the M1 are not symmetrical.

00:21:12.830 --> 00:21:14.680
This comes with some drawbacks.

00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:19.309
The leading road wheel will hit bumps first,
meaning it will absorb more force than the

00:21:19.309 --> 00:21:21.150
trailing road wheel.

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:26.510
Increasing the wear on the leading torsion
spring, resulting in more frequent replacement.

00:21:26.510 --> 00:21:31.390
Torsion bars are very easy to swap out when
they aren’t broken, but if bent or shattered,

00:21:31.390 --> 00:21:36.260
or if the body of the tank is bent, it can
be difficult to pull the long torsion springs

00:21:36.260 --> 00:21:37.270
out.

00:21:37.270 --> 00:21:41.299
Modern tanks are increasingly moving towards
hydrogas suspensions.

00:21:41.299 --> 00:21:42.760
Which look like this.

00:21:42.760 --> 00:21:47.720
The road wheel has an attached axle pivot
arm, which in turn turns a crank and con-rod

00:21:47.720 --> 00:21:48.830
piston.

00:21:48.830 --> 00:21:51.950
This piston connects to the hydragas suspension
cylinder.

00:21:51.950 --> 00:21:55.570
The first chamber is filled with a fluid,
typically oil.

00:21:55.570 --> 00:22:00.821
The oil is relatively incompressible, and
so provides marginal spring force, but this

00:22:00.821 --> 00:22:05.520
chamber has a damper valve that serves to
restrict the transfer of fluid between this

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:11.170
chamber and the next, which contains a floating
piston with a compressed nitrogen gas on the

00:22:11.170 --> 00:22:12.380
other side.

00:22:12.380 --> 00:22:17.011
This provides resistance to ensure the road
wheels stay in contact with the ground, but

00:22:17.011 --> 00:22:18.420
also dampens out vibrations.

00:22:18.420 --> 00:22:19.470
[REF] [9]

00:22:19.470 --> 00:22:24.620
This system is much smaller, lighter and easier
to service than a torsion bar, while lowering

00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:28.840
the height of the tank, lowering its profile
and making it harder to hit.

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:34.059
Not only that, but each individual suspension
can be remotely adjusted by changing the gas

00:22:34.059 --> 00:22:36.080
pressure inside the cylinder.

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:39.559
Allowing the tank to take a crouched position
if needed.

00:22:39.559 --> 00:22:44.490
Hydrogas suspensions were considered for the
M1 during development, but it was a relatively

00:22:44.490 --> 00:22:48.450
new technology at the time, and so the torsion
spring was chosen.

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:49.450
[REF][9]

00:22:49.450 --> 00:22:54.290
The M1 Abrams has had incremental improvements
added over the past 4 decades.

00:22:54.290 --> 00:23:01.460
With three primary variants the M1, M1A1 and
M1A2, with even more specialized iterations

00:23:01.460 --> 00:23:02.460
in between.

00:23:02.460 --> 00:23:07.029
With updated sensors and controls to bring
the tank into the 21st century.

00:23:07.029 --> 00:23:10.190
Like the Crows II on the M1A2 SEP.

00:23:10.190 --> 00:23:13.370
SEP standing for systems enhancement package.

00:23:13.370 --> 00:23:17.860
Which added additional thermal sights for
the tank commander, an auxiliary power unit

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:22.690
to run it’s electronics without running
the fuel hungry engine, and the CROWS II controller.

00:23:22.690 --> 00:23:27.169
A remote control sight that allows the tank
commander to operate the gun from the safety

00:23:27.169 --> 00:23:28.169
of the turret.

00:23:28.169 --> 00:23:29.169
[REF][10]

00:23:29.169 --> 00:23:34.640
We are currently on the M1A2 SEP V4 variant,
and the army is considering the future of

00:23:34.640 --> 00:23:41.200
the M1 program, whether that’s an M1A2 SEP
V5, or a completely new platform, but make

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:47.150
no mistake, despite the M1 being one of longest
serving tanks in a modern military, over 4

00:23:47.150 --> 00:23:52.550
decades old it’s still highly capable and
it will be a massive asset to the troops in

00:23:52.550 --> 00:23:54.540
the ground in Ukraine.

